WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:05.780
In a tweet thread exchanged with JetBlue on
April 17th, writer Mackenzie Fegan had a few

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:08.660
questions about the airline’s new boarding
procedure.

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:09.660
No boarding pass.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:10.660
No ID.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:16.330
Instead, a camera and screen verified her
identity against a US Customs and Border Patrol

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:18.840
database, then let her on the plane.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.820
Some passengers might consider the increasing
use of facial recognition in everyday life

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:26.960
convenient, some might think it’s Orwellian,
but it’s already everywhere.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.820
The question is, how far will it go?

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:33.769
Turns out, even some of those developing the
technology are scared of what the answer might

00:00:33.769 --> 00:00:34.989
be.

00:00:34.989 --> 00:00:39.469
This is your Bloomberg QuickTake on Facial
Recognition.

00:00:39.469 --> 00:00:44.199
In May, San Francisco became the first American
city to block police and other agencies from

00:00:44.199 --> 00:00:46.010
using facial recognition software.

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:49.819
The biggest concerns are really
around civil liberties and whether you're

00:00:49.819 --> 00:00:56.131
essentially enabling a kind of totalitarian
state with this technology, which sort of

00:00:56.131 --> 00:00:57.131
seems to be the way things are heading.

00:00:57.131 --> 00:00:58.760
There are concerns about the use of this technology
by law enforcement and by police.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:00.239
How’d we get here?

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:05.010
Like other artificial intelligence applications,
facial recognition initially developed slowly,

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:06.010
beginning in the 1960s.

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:10.740
Then around 2012, with the help of newly available
high-def cameras, machine learning, and giant

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:14.869
databases of photos to increase accuracy,
it advanced in a hurry.

00:01:14.869 --> 00:01:21.710
facial recognition takes images
from video cameras and tries to identify the

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:23.469
faces of people in those images.

00:01:23.469 --> 00:01:28.460
it does so by taking some key points 
in the face usually and doing measurements

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:31.869
of the distance between all those various
points.

00:01:31.869 --> 00:01:36.350
In December 2018, London police made their
first arrest based on facial recognition,

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:40.930
after cross-checking photos of pedestrians
in tourist hotspots against a database of

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:42.180
known criminals.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:48.049
In New Delhi, a police trial reportedly identified
3,000 missing children in just four days.

00:01:48.049 --> 00:01:52.020
So if it involves catching criminals and finding
missing children, why would anyone be against

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.020
it?

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:57.210
For that, one might look at the most developed
facial recognition network in the world, in

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:58.210
China.

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:03.030
CCTV and facial recognition have
been used in combination to create this sort

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:07.009
of vast surveillance apparatus and that's
been particularly applied against certain

00:02:07.009 --> 00:02:10.710
ethnic minority groups, but even in western
democracies people are concerned about police

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:17.440
departments using this technology to try to
find suspects, or even people who might be

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:21.930
involved in legitimate protests, so they could
be tracked.

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:25.800
And those are just concerns about the technology
when it works as intended.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:30.090
A study from the MIT Media Lab found that
white men in a sample were correctly identified

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:35.159
99 percent of the time, while error rates
of up to 35% were found when it came to darker

00:02:35.159 --> 00:02:36.159
skinned women.

00:02:36.159 --> 00:02:39.330
Microsoft came out last year.

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:45.659
It was the first major tech company to say,
they really feel uncomfortable with deploying

00:02:45.659 --> 00:02:48.470
this technology until there's clear regulation
around it.

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:53.280
They were then joined by Amazon, which seconded
those calls.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:59.060
It seems like some other companies are kind
of plowing ahead without such qualms at all.

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:03.590
To stake out some guidelines, the Algorithmic
Justice Center and Georgetown University Law

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:08.990
Center unveiled the Safe Face Pledge, which
asks companies not to provide facial AI for

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:14.189
autonomous weapons or sell to law enforcement
unless explicit laws are debated and passed

00:03:14.189 --> 00:03:15.189
to allow it.

00:03:15.189 --> 00:03:20.030
A few companies have signed on, but notably
not Microsoft or Amazon, possibly loathe to

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:24.340
lose the opportunity to sell facial recognition
to police departments and governments the

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:26.110
world over.

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:30.950
So what's preventing your image from saying
a whole lot more about you than it used to?

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:32.400
Face it, not much.

